WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:18.120
Welcome to a public affair and WRT 89.9 FM. This is Monday edition of the program hosted

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:27.080
by Yuri Rashkin. Today the subject of our conversation is torture. It is not a subject

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:32.880
that is pleasing and it's a difficult conversation, but I think one that needs to be had when we

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:38.200
have good reason for it. Here's the situation. We're going to be spending today's program

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:42.800
reviewing, doing something unusual that I don't think this program or most programs

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:49.360
ever really do. We're going to be concentrating not necessarily on a book, but on a review

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:55.360
of a book. And so here's the situation. Well, the Navy Institute has published a

00:00:55.360 --> 00:01:01.440
book called The CIA Response to the Senate Intelligence Committee's Study of its Detention

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:09.240
and Interrogation Program. With such an exciting title, this includes contributors,

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:14.120
include former CAE Director George Tenet, Porter Goss, Michael Hayden, former Deputy

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:19.160
Director John McLaughlin, and Michael Morrill, former Counterterrorist, Center Deputy Director

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:24.160
Jay Philip Mudd, former Chief Legal Counsel John Rizzo, and former head of clandestine

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:29.720
service Jose Rodriguez, Jr. Well, that sounds like an interesting book right there, doesn't

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:35.240
it? At this point, I'm excited to welcome to the program my guest is today for this

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:41.880
hour is John Kuriyaku. And it's nice when you have a Wikipedia page dedicated to

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:46.960
your guest so that you can look up exactly, see, you know, who you are speaking to.

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:52.960
And so I will read a little bit from John's Wikipedia page. He was a CAE analyst and

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:57.800
case officer, senior investigator for the Senate Foreign Relations Committee and counterterrorism

00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:02.720
consultant for ABC News and author. He was the first year as government official to confirm

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:08.280
in December 2007 that waterboarding was used to interrogate Al Qaeda prisoners, which he

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:14.280
described as torture. On October 22nd, 2012, Kuriyaku pleaded guilty to disclosing classified

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:18.120
information about a fellow CAE officer that connected the covert operative to a

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:23.000
specific operation. He was the first person to pass classified information to a reporter,

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:28.200
although the reporter did not publish the name of the operative. John Kuriyaku, welcome

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:32.360
to a public affair. How are you doing? Thank you so much for having me. I'm doing very

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:37.120
well. Thank you. It's really exciting to have you on the program. So we have a

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:43.920
book coming out and the book basically is just if it is their response to a Senate

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:49.660
report that says there was torture and was it, you know, there were no results to show

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:56.040
its effectiveness. Yeah, and I would remind listeners as well that the Senate report, whether

00:02:56.040 --> 00:03:04.280
or not it had Republican support, was irrelevant. It was based solely on primary source documentation.

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:10.720
That is contemporaneous CIA reporting from the field. This was information that came

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:16.720
from the horse's mouth. And with that in mind, do you feel that the information is

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:21.040
reliable? And what are your thoughts on the book that's going to be put out here

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:27.620
shortly by all the CAE directors? Well, I do believe that the Senate report was

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:33.160
reliable and it was entirely consistent with the CIA Inspector General's report that

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:39.960
was written in 2005 and released in 2009. That report also found that torture did

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:46.840
not work, that actionable intelligence was not collected, and that no attacks were

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:53.000
disrupted and no American lives were saved by torture. How can we prove the opposite, you know?

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:58.880
Right. How do you prove a negative? And so because of that, we have this book that's

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:06.720
come out written by three former CIA directors, as you said, and a bevy of

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:11.280
other senior, former senior officials. But my position is that the reason that they've

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:15.680
written this book is not necessarily to refute the Senate torture report, but to

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:21.240
try to save their own reputation. The torture program is their legacy. It's

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:26.280
what history will know them by. And I think that they think that if they keep

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:31.720
repeating the lie that torture worked over and over and over again, that the

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:38.720
American people will be either too gullible or too stupid to appreciate the truth.

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:43.720
Is that a matter of public perception or legal liability, do you think?

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:48.360
I think it's really a matter of public perception because for many of the crimes

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:53.720
outlined in the Senate torture report, the statutes of limitations have expired.

00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:58.280
So I think this is really about these men's place in history.

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:03.840
Listeners, give us a call. What your thoughts are on torture. Do you feel this is

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:08.160
something that is justified and something that ever works? Give us a call.

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:14.240
608-256-2001. And frankly, I think considering our guest, I'd like to

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:18.440
specifically hear from people who feel that torture is justified and is

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:22.680
effective in certain situations because it's always nice to have a couple

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:25.520
of different points of view. John, can you talk a little bit about your

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:32.080
background with CIA? Sure. I spent just under 15 years with the CIA. I spent the

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:35.160
first half of that career as an analyst working on the Middle East and

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:40.880
primarily on Iraq. Served overseas in that capacity as well. And then in 1997 I

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:46.760
switched to counterterrorism operations, working to recruit spies to

00:05:46.760 --> 00:05:51.560
steal secrets and to infiltrate groups like Al Qaeda to disrupt future

00:05:51.560 --> 00:05:56.440
terrorist attacks. After September 11th I became the chief of CIA counterterrorism

00:05:56.440 --> 00:06:03.720
operations in Pakistan. And I led a raid, I led a series of raids in March 2002 that

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:08.720
resulted in the capture of Abu Zubaydah, who at the time we believed was the

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:11.800
number three in Al Qaeda. That turned out to not be correct, although he was a

00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:15.920
very senior logistician and he created and maintained Al Qaeda's

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:22.760
training camps in southern Afghanistan. I left the CIA in 2004, went into the

00:06:22.760 --> 00:06:29.000
private sector for five years, and then I became the senior investigator on the

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:34.880
Senate Foreign Relations Committee. Everything changed for me at least in

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:41.800
2012. In 2007 I went on ABC News and I gave an interview to Brian Ross and I

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:47.840
said three things. I said that the CIA was torturing its prisoners, that torture

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:53.520
was official U.S. government policy, and that the policy had been approved of and

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:58.520
signed by the president. And so the FBI began investigating me. The

00:06:58.520 --> 00:07:01.880
investigation lasted four years. I didn't know I was under investigation.

00:07:01.880 --> 00:07:07.120
And in January of 2012 I was charged with five felonies, including three

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:12.320
counts of espionage coming out of that interview. Four of the charges were

00:07:12.320 --> 00:07:17.200
finally dropped and I took a plea to a lesser charge of violating the

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:22.920
Intelligence Identities Protection Act of 1982. I was sentenced to 30 months in

00:07:22.920 --> 00:07:27.320
prison. I served 23 those months and was released earlier this year.

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:33.080
What is your opinion of the Obama administration and its efforts on dealing

00:07:33.080 --> 00:07:37.520
not just with terrorism but with torture? Well, I think the Obama

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:44.040
administration really has done very little to address the torture issue. It

00:07:44.040 --> 00:07:50.080
wasn't until just this past spring that Diane Feinstein, the senior senator from

00:07:50.080 --> 00:07:56.760
California and John McCain, Republican of Arizona, joined together to sponsor a

00:07:56.760 --> 00:08:00.360
bill that was very quickly passed into law that would finally and

00:08:00.440 --> 00:08:08.520
definitively ban torture by U.S. government entities. What President

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:14.200
Obama did when he was elected president is find an executive order banning

00:08:14.200 --> 00:08:18.880
torture, but that didn't necessarily have the effect of law in that any

00:08:18.880 --> 00:08:22.600
future president could have just signed an executive order

00:08:22.600 --> 00:08:26.680
re-implementing the torture program. So what we really needed was a

00:08:26.680 --> 00:08:30.120
law and the impetus for that law did not come from the White House. It

00:08:30.120 --> 00:08:38.040
came from the Congress. And now we have a law. Are people going to be held

00:08:38.040 --> 00:08:43.400
responsible? Well, that's really the $64,000 question and I think the answer

00:08:43.400 --> 00:08:48.640
is clearly now no. No one will be held responsible. As I said a few minutes ago,

00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:54.480
the statute of limitations on many of these crimes have expired and the

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:58.800
president has made it clear that he wants to, as he put it, look forward

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:05.080
not backward and nobody involved in torture. Even those officers who killed

00:09:05.080 --> 00:09:09.720
prisoners during the course of interrogation, even they will not be

00:09:09.720 --> 00:09:15.960
held responsible for their crimes. What message do you feel the sends? Well,

00:09:15.960 --> 00:09:21.800
it's just so hypocritical of us. The State Department is mandated every year

00:09:21.800 --> 00:09:26.560
with writing a human rights report on every country with which we have

00:09:26.600 --> 00:09:32.120
diplomatic relations. And I was an embassy human rights officer when I was

00:09:32.120 --> 00:09:35.520
serving in the Middle East and we would go to the Ministry of Justice and the

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:38.720
Ministry of Interior and to the office of the Prime Minister and we would

00:09:38.720 --> 00:09:43.160
complain about human rights abuses that we had documented. And we would tell

00:09:43.160 --> 00:09:48.840
them, look, you risk having a cough of American aid. You risk having

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:51.920
military aid cut off if you don't improve your human rights practices.

00:09:52.440 --> 00:09:57.960
Well, in fact, we were doing things that were much worse than most of these

00:09:57.960 --> 00:09:58.880
other countries did.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:06.500
So it's very hypocritical of us to talk about human rights and to profess to be this shining

00:10:06.500 --> 00:10:12.540
beacon on a hill, you know, who's supporting and demanding around the world respect for

00:10:12.540 --> 00:10:16.300
human rights when we don't respect human rights.

00:10:16.300 --> 00:10:18.380
And I'm talking here only about torture.

00:10:18.380 --> 00:10:24.000
I'm not talking about things like solitary confinement or the death penalty or other

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:25.000
domestic issues.

00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:31.000
I'm talking specifically about torturing prisoners in secret prisons overseas.

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:33.000
See, this is the thing that...

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:40.000
The thing that I think most of us human beings who live in a lot of comfort don't think about

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:45.000
is I think how little it takes for a human being to feel tortured.

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:47.000
And that's legitimate.

00:10:47.000 --> 00:10:51.000
I mean, you can leave a light bulb on in a room with no windows 24 hours a day.

00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:53.000
That's torture.

00:10:53.000 --> 00:10:55.000
I agree with you.

00:10:55.000 --> 00:10:59.000
You know, in fact, we talk so much about waterboarding.

00:10:59.000 --> 00:11:01.000
And certainly waterboarding is a terrible thing.

00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:07.000
But the CIA was using techniques that I believe were even worse than waterboarding on prisoners.

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:11.000
One of those was sleep deprivation.

00:11:11.000 --> 00:11:16.000
And as you say, they leave bright lights on 24 hours a day in a windowless cell.

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:20.000
They bombard the cell with hard rock music.

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:25.000
I'm not talking about forcing a prisoner to be awake for 24 hours or 48 or 72.

00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:32.000
I'm talking about forcing a prisoner to remain awake for 10 days, 12, 14 days even.

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:38.000
We know now that some prisoners lose their minds to the point where they're not able

00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:40.000
to participate in their own defenses.

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:42.000
And to me, that's torture.

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:48.000
And another thing that we were doing, and this actually resulted in the death of two prisoners,

00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:52.000
was what's called the cold cell, where we strip a prisoner naked.

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:57.000
We chain him to an eyeball in the ceiling so he can't sit or kneel.

00:11:57.000 --> 00:12:04.000
We chill the cell to 50 degrees, and then every hour we throw ice water on him.

00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:06.000
Well, that kills you.

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:09.000
Your body can't take that kind of hypothermia.

00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:15.000
And how George Tennant and Mike Hayden and Porter Goss and their minions

00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:20.000
think that this is torture is just a mystery to me.

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:23.000
You're listening to WRT, Monday edition of a public affair.

00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:29.000
This is Yuri Rashkin and my guest is former CIA counterterrorism officer John Kiriako.

00:12:29.000 --> 00:12:33.000
On a lighter note, I think one of the forms of torture is to listen to talk radio

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:35.000
and not be able to participate.

00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:38.000
On that note, I'm excited to welcome to program a caller.

00:12:38.000 --> 00:12:43.000
Thomas, what's your question or comment for our guest?

00:12:43.000 --> 00:12:44.000
Hello?

00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:46.000
Yes. My name is Thomas.

00:12:46.000 --> 00:12:47.000
Yes.

00:12:47.000 --> 00:12:54.000
Okay. When you called up, you said you wanted to hear from people who think torture works.

00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:55.000
Absolutely.

00:12:55.000 --> 00:12:58.000
And I'm going to give you my opinion on that.

00:12:58.000 --> 00:13:05.000
The reason torture works is because it puts fear into all the other prisoners

00:13:05.000 --> 00:13:11.000
and it puts fear into the American people so it's easier for them to go to war.

00:13:11.000 --> 00:13:21.000
And the second reason that works is because then we can say we have prevented,

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:26.000
we have prevented espionage in this country

00:13:26.000 --> 00:13:31.000
or we have prevented an attack by terrorists in this country

00:13:31.000 --> 00:13:36.000
and therefore in order to preserve our way of life,

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:40.000
we have to get all the information on everybody that lives here

00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:43.000
and that's how we're keeping you free.

00:13:43.000 --> 00:13:46.000
And so torture works in that sense.

00:13:46.000 --> 00:13:50.000
Thomas, it almost sounds a little like you're being a little sarcastic here, I guess, to me.

00:13:50.000 --> 00:13:52.000
I am not being sarcastic.

00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:59.000
I'm trying to tell you that's the way it works on the general population

00:13:59.000 --> 00:14:02.000
and even some of the people who do the torturing.

00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:04.000
So I'm not being sarcastic.

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:08.000
So you're saying it has a certain effect and that's the effect that it has?

00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:09.000
Yes.

00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:10.000
Right?

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:13.000
It's easy to do and thank you for the program.

00:14:13.000 --> 00:14:15.000
Thank you very much for the call.

00:14:15.000 --> 00:14:17.000
John, what are your thoughts?

00:14:17.000 --> 00:14:22.000
Well, you know, that's, in a funny way, I have to agree with the caller

00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:28.000
because it does have a way of cowing the American people.

00:14:28.000 --> 00:14:35.000
You know, when former Attorney General Eric Holder went before the Senate Judiciary Committee

00:14:35.000 --> 00:14:40.000
and was asked by, I think it was Senator Rand Paul,

00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:45.000
does the President have the right to murder American citizens with drones?

00:14:45.000 --> 00:14:49.000
And Attorney General Holder said yes.

00:14:49.000 --> 00:14:55.000
And then the Senator asked, does he have the right to murder American citizens with drones on U.S. territory?

00:14:55.000 --> 00:14:59.000
And the Attorney General wouldn't respond to that question.

00:14:59.000 --> 00:15:03.000
Then I think that what the caller is saying is probably true.

00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:10.000
We are frightened into following our leadership like Lemmings right off the edge of the cliff.

00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:15.000
And I think that many of our leaders probably want that to be the case.

00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:19.000
If we presume that torture doesn't work to get information

00:15:19.000 --> 00:15:22.000
and you worked for Central Intelligence Agency,

00:15:22.000 --> 00:15:26.000
what is an effective way of getting information out of people in your opinion?

00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:30.000
Well, as much as CIA officers hate the FBI institutionally,

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:33.000
going back to the creation of the CIA in the 1940s,

00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:36.000
we have to give credit where credit is due.

00:15:36.000 --> 00:15:41.000
The FBI really is the federal government's elite interrogation force.

00:15:41.000 --> 00:15:44.000
They really know how to do it and to do it successfully.

00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:47.000
And the way you do it successfully is to build a rapport

00:15:47.000 --> 00:15:51.000
and a relationship with the person that you're interrogating.

00:15:51.000 --> 00:15:56.000
This takes time, but the FBI and military interrogators

00:15:56.000 --> 00:16:01.000
succeeded wildly with this method in the immediate aftermath of the Second World War

00:16:01.000 --> 00:16:06.000
with German scientists and German military leaders.

00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:10.000
We should have done that in the so-called war on terror.

00:16:10.000 --> 00:16:15.000
Instead, we were impatient and we believed that we could get the same kind of information

00:16:15.000 --> 00:16:19.000
through torture that we could have through rapport building.

00:16:19.000 --> 00:16:21.000
And frankly, we didn't.

00:16:21.000 --> 00:16:23.000
All right, we have a caller on the line.

00:16:23.000 --> 00:16:25.000
Keith, welcome to the program.

00:16:25.000 --> 00:16:28.000
What is your question or comment for our guest?

00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:32.000
Oh, hi, yes, it's a great honor to speak with John.

00:16:32.000 --> 00:16:34.000
Thank you.

00:16:34.000 --> 00:16:43.000
I'm wondering if you had decided whether you actually thought about going public with this very long

00:16:43.000 --> 00:16:49.000
and did you ever imagine that you would be prosecuted for actually revealing this information?

00:16:49.000 --> 00:16:54.000
And did you ever think about doing what Edward Snowden did and leaving in...

00:16:54.000 --> 00:16:56.000
You know, I did not give this...

00:16:56.000 --> 00:17:00.000
I'm embarrassed to say I did not give this a lot of thought before I did it.

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:05.000
Brian Ross called me in December 2007 and said that he had a source

00:17:05.000 --> 00:17:08.000
who said that I had tortured Abu Zubeda.

00:17:08.000 --> 00:17:11.000
I said that that was absolutely untrue.

00:17:11.000 --> 00:17:14.000
I was the only person who was kind to Abu Zubeda.

00:17:14.000 --> 00:17:16.000
I sat with him next to his bed.

00:17:16.000 --> 00:17:18.000
I held his hand.

00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:20.000
We talked about our lives.

00:17:20.000 --> 00:17:24.000
I never laid a hand on Abu Zubeda or on any other prisoner.

00:17:24.000 --> 00:17:28.000
And Brian Ross said, well, you're free to come on to the show and defend yourself.

00:17:28.000 --> 00:17:31.000
And I was weighing whether or not I should do this.

00:17:31.000 --> 00:17:36.000
And at that same time, President Bush gave a press conference

00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:41.000
in which he looked right at the camera and he said, we do not torture.

00:17:41.000 --> 00:17:43.000
Well, I knew that that was a lie.

00:17:43.000 --> 00:17:45.000
We had been torturing people for years.

00:17:45.000 --> 00:17:50.000
And then later in the week, President Bush said, if there is torture,

00:17:50.000 --> 00:17:53.000
it's the result of a rogue CIA officer.

00:17:53.000 --> 00:17:56.000
And I thought, oh my God, they're going to pimp on me.

00:17:56.000 --> 00:17:59.000
So I called Brian Ross and I agreed to the interview.

00:17:59.000 --> 00:18:04.000
And when he asked me a direct question about whether or not we were torturing prisoners,

00:18:04.000 --> 00:18:07.000
I gave him a direct answer and I said yes.

00:18:07.000 --> 00:18:11.000
When I was first arrested, I went to see an attorney,

00:18:11.000 --> 00:18:16.000
Jeff Lynn Radak, who specializes in whistleblower defense.

00:18:16.000 --> 00:18:20.000
And I said to her, listen, I should tell you off the bat, I'm not a whistleblower.

00:18:20.000 --> 00:18:22.000
And she said, oh yes, you are.

00:18:22.000 --> 00:18:24.000
She said, no whistleblower thinks he's a whistleblower.

00:18:24.000 --> 00:18:27.000
But there's a legal definition of whistleblowing.

00:18:27.000 --> 00:18:31.000
And it's bringing to light any evidence of waste, fraud, abuse or illegality.

00:18:31.000 --> 00:18:34.000
She said, you're the poster boy for whistleblowers.

00:18:34.000 --> 00:18:36.000
So I decided to fight.

00:18:36.000 --> 00:18:40.000
And I made a decision that I was going to go trial, which I ended up not doing.

00:18:40.000 --> 00:18:43.000
But I fought for an entire year.

00:18:43.000 --> 00:18:48.000
I didn't know it at the time, but Ed Snowden was closely following my case.

00:18:48.000 --> 00:18:54.000
And then after I took a plea and went to prison, Snowden made his revelations.

