put this behind you?

00:50:38.260 --> 00:50:41.660
Yeah, you know, I really hate being the torture guy,

00:50:41.660 --> 00:50:44.620
although that seems to be what I've become.

00:50:44.620 --> 00:50:49.180
I'm working now at the Institute for Policy Studies, a highly respected

00:50:49.180 --> 00:50:50.820
and

00:50:50.820 --> 00:50:53.300
old think tank here in Washington,

00:50:53.300 --> 00:50:57.260
looking at issues like torture and intelligence community reform and prison

00:50:57.260 --> 00:50:58.500
reform even.

00:50:58.500 --> 00:51:00.700
So I intend to stay there

00:51:00.700 --> 00:51:02.260
and I've been speaking

00:51:02.260 --> 00:51:06.600
around the country, mostly at colleges and universities, but also before peace

00:51:06.600 --> 00:51:08.540
groups and a couple of non-profits.

00:51:08.540 --> 00:51:11.300
So I hope to continue doing that as well.

00:51:11.300 --> 00:51:16.940
And I'm going to teach a class in the ethics of intelligence operations

00:51:16.940 --> 00:51:19.500
that I developed at the university level.

00:51:19.500 --> 00:51:20.900
And I've got two books

00:51:20.900 --> 00:51:24.500
getting ready to come out. One is called Letters from Loretto,

00:51:24.500 --> 00:51:29.340
which is based on a series of blog posts that I wrote from prison.

00:51:29.340 --> 00:51:31.260
And the next is called

00:51:31.260 --> 00:51:36.100
Doing Time Like a Spy, How the CIA Taught Me to Survive and Thrive in Prison,

00:51:36.100 --> 00:51:38.260
which is sort of a more fun

00:51:38.260 --> 00:51:39.460
book full of

00:51:39.460 --> 00:51:40.660
anecdotes.

00:51:40.660 --> 00:51:43.700
So kind of not your worst, you know, what to do in a worst case scenario

00:51:43.700 --> 00:51:46.620
about how to get through life, you know, how to think like Einstein or how

00:51:46.620 --> 00:51:48.500
to think like a CIA spy.

00:51:48.500 --> 00:51:50.900
That's right. You know, when you go to prison,

00:51:50.900 --> 00:51:55.500
the first thing you want to do is make sure that you stay at the top of the social heap.

00:51:55.500 --> 00:52:00.620
That sounds like most any organization, doesn't it?

00:52:00.620 --> 00:52:05.420
John, I want to thank you for being on the program today. John Kiriakou is

00:52:05.420 --> 00:52:09.780
an individual who's been through a lot and I very much appreciate his ability

00:52:09.780 --> 00:52:15.140
to connect with us today and share his experience and answer your calls.

00:52:15.180 --> 00:52:18.460
You're listening to WRT Public Affair.

00:52:18.460 --> 00:52:20.220
This is Yuri Rashkin

00:52:20.220 --> 00:52:23.500
and I want to also thank Holly for producing, for engineering the program

00:52:23.500 --> 00:52:24.940
and Sarah for

00:52:24.940 --> 00:52:25.900
engineering,

00:52:25.900 --> 00:52:30.900
producing. Take care.

